{"id":"m2://rec/open_logic/content/methods/proofs/reading-proofs.tex#proof-1#1","source":"open_logic","snapshot":"blake3:a5eb116529d3e799289dd9f7e7530d0d446b3615f5608fe08c668241eee3c765","locator":"https://github.com/OpenLogicProject/OpenLogic/blob/1e960beff9ed7835bf3e3f1335e21af3439cd107/content/methods/proofs/reading-proofs.tex#L28","native_id":"content/methods/proofs/reading-proofs.tex#proof-1","kind":"proof","text":"If $z \\in A \\cap (A \\cup B)$, then $z \\in A$, so $A \\cap (A \\cup B)\n\\subseteq A$. Now suppose $z \\in A$. Then also $z \\in A \\cup B$, and\ntherefore also $z \\in A \\cap (A \\cup B)$.","class":"open","by":"textbook/1","resource":"PKGMM3P5QY","at":{"section":"45.1","kind":"proof"}}
